    <!-- Dynamic Cosserat-rod actuation: converts a commanded 3-tendon
         length vector into tensions, solves the static Cosserat BVP for
         the resulting backbone shape, and drives every joint above toward
         the resulting per-segment relative-rotation target with a PD
         force loop (see CosseratArmController.hh for the full pipeline).

         use_guided_tendons=true selects CosseratRodSolver::SolveGuided()
         (3 discrete routing notches at 1/3, 2/3, 1 of the rod, matching
         the hardware's own internal routing points) over the plain
         Solve() this replaced. Two earlier tendon-loading models were
         tried and rejected first: a load concentrated only at the tip,
         and a UNIFORM per-unit-length load smeared along the whole rod
         (distribute_tendon_load_continuously) - both put the tendon's
         force through a small-curvature approximation (each point's pull
         assumed to stay parallel to the rod's LOCAL +z, regardless of how
         much the rod had actually bent by that point), and BOTH were live-
         verified to genuinely buckle into a non-single-curvature,
         multi-lobe elastica shape once tension climbed past a modest
         threshold (a few N) - a real post-buckling mode (per-joint
         curvature magnitude oscillating with near-zero dips between
         clearly-curved regions), not a solver-convergence artifact, and
         independent of youngs_modulus/shear_modulus (swept 1.6e5-1e8 Pa
         offline with a standalone solver-only driver - buckling tension
         and required bending tension scale together regardless of
         material stiffness, so raising either constant alone can't help).
         An even earlier discrete-3-notch attempt using that SAME
         fixed-local-frame approximation at each notch made things WORSE,
         not better - independently-approximated points geometrically
         interfered with each other instead of reinforcing one consistent
         pull, which is exactly what SolveGuided() fixes.

         SolveGuided() replaces the fixed-local-frame guess with a
         self-consistent outer loop: solve the BVP, read back where each
         notch (offset from the backbone by the routing radius, per
         tendon) actually ended up, recompute that tendon's force at each
         notch from the ACTUAL chord direction to its neighbors (a
         frictionless-guide idealization - tension pulls toward whichever
         neighboring anchor on each side), and re-solve, iterating (damped,
         relax=0.25) to a fixed point. As the rod bends, this makes each
         notch's pull progressively MORE lateral (bending-inducing) and
         LESS axially-compressive than the fixed-+z guess assumed - a
         self-reinforcing effect that a standalone offline sweep (bypassing
         gz entirely, see CosseratRodSolver.hpp) showed keeps the shape
         genuinely single-curvature (zero oscillation dips) all the way
         past 120deg aggregate bend at single-digit-N tension, vs. the
         small-curvature model's ~12-15deg ceiling before onset of
         buckling. This is the best current explanation for why the real
         hardware (well-routed physical tendons naturally track this same
         chord geometry) reaches high bend angles that the old
         small-curvature approximation's simulated buckling collapse could
         never reproduce - not a case of the real arm being made of a
         stiffer material, since E/G tuning alone was verified not to move
         the small-curvature model's ceiling at all.

         tendon_stiffness/max_tension were rebalanced for how much LESS
         tension SolveGuided() needs for the same bend (~2.5-3N for 65deg
         single-tendon, vs. the small-curvature model needing ~100N+ for
         the same target and still buckling well before reaching it);
         max_tension=10N keeps normal operation safely inside the
         confirmed-clean region with headroom for multi-tendon combined
         commands (which can concentrate more tension on one tendon than
         an equivalent single-axis command). tendon_baseline stays at 0
         (a nonzero baseline was found, in the small-curvature-model
         investigation, to shrink the clean window by adding constant
         axial preload even at rest; not re-tested under SolveGuided() but
         kept at the conservative known-safe value). continuum_arm_pcc's
         kinematic model has no notion of axial tendon load at all, so its
         own reachable range was always a ceiling-free idealization - the
         point of this fix is closing the gap to it, not that PCC itself
         changed.

         youngs_modulus/shear_modulus/solidity_fill_factor (2026-08):
         real hardware photos show a rigid-plastic (PLA/PETG-class) crossed-
         strut lattice wall, not a continuously solid tube wall and not a
         soft silicone/TPU material - the arm's compliance comes from the
         lattice's sparse GEOMETRY, not a soft base material. solidity_fill_
         factor=0.20 scales A/Ixx/Iyy/J in CosseratRodSolver's constructor
         to model that ~80%-open wall (see CosseratRodSolver.hpp's own
         comment); youngs_modulus is now the strut MATERIAL's own modulus
         rather than a pre-softened "effective" value. Value derived (not
         measured) to reproduce the PCC model's own joint stiffness baseline
         K_theta~=1.611 N*m/rad via K_theta=EI/L: with fill_factor=0.20,
         I_eff = 0.20*(pi/4)*(0.02^4-0.015^4) = 1.718e-8 m^4, target
         EI = 1.611*0.4 = 0.6444 N*m^2 => E = 0.6444/1.718e-8 ~= 3.75e7 Pa -
         squarely inside a rigid-plastic-at-sparse-fill range and inside the
         1e7-1e8 Pa range hardware photos motivated. shear_modulus scaled to
         keep the prior E/G~=3.125 ratio. NOTE: the buckling investigation
         above found raising E alone doesn't fix buckling under the OLD
         small-curvature Solve() path (buckling tension and required
         bending tension scale together there) - that finding doesn't
         directly apply here since SolveGuided() (active, use_guided_tendons
         =true) doesn't exhibit that ceiling in the first place, but the
         ~15x net EI increase (0.20 fill x 75x E) means tendon_stiffness/
         max_tension/effort_limit below (tuned for the old, ~15x softer EI)
         need to be re-verified against real reachability, not assumed
         still valid - see the live verification this value's introduction
         was accompanied by. -->
    <plugin filename="CosseratArmController" name="cosserat_arm_controller::CosseratArmController">
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_1</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_2</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_3</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_4</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_5</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_6</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_7</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_8</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_9</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_10</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_11</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_12</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_13</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_14</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_15</joint_name>
      <rod_length>0.4</rod_length>
      <rod_outer_radius>0.02</rod_outer_radius>
      <rod_inner_radius>0.015</rod_inner_radius>
      <youngs_modulus>3.75e7</youngs_modulus>
      <shear_modulus>1.2e7</shear_modulus>
      <solidity_fill_factor>0.20</solidity_fill_factor>
      <rod_density>780.1713</rod_density>
      <pitch_radius>0.015</pitch_radius>
      <!-- tendon_stiffness/max_tension/effort_limit/position gains
           re-derived and LIVE-VERIFIED (2026-08 open-loop retune, gz bench
           harness, not just the standalone solver) against the same
           75deg-in-2.0s/zero-overshoot open-loop target as
           continuum_arm_pcc. The PD loop here has NO passive spring backup
           (unlike PCC's real per-joint spring_stiffness) - all restoring/
           holding torque comes from position_p_gain/position_d_gain alone,
           so this model needed materially higher gains (8.0/0.10 vs PCC's
           5.0/0.05) and a higher max_tension (120N vs the Phase-2-audit's
           100N) to reach the same ~75deg target through the full solve+PD
           pipeline, not just the solver in isolation. tendon_stiffness
           unchanged (3333 N/m); effort_limit kept at the max_tension*
           pitch_radius convention: 120*0.015=1.8 N*m.
           IMPORTANT measurement gotcha found live: gz's real_time_factor
           drops measurably below 1.0 as commanded tension approaches
           max_tension (SolveGuided()'s outer loop costs more per call at
           higher tension even though it still converges cleanly, no
           warnings) - a wall-clock-timed step-response test then reports
           an inflated settling time that has nothing to do with the
           plant's actual dynamics. Bench verification must sample against
           gz's own sim time (world stats topic), not python time.time() -
           see gz_arm_harness.py's sim_time()/real_time_factor(). This RTF
           drop is itself a real, reportable Task-3 compute-benchmark
           finding (Cosserat costs more CPU than PCC/RBSN, worst at high
           bend), not a bug to hide. -->
      <tendon_stiffness>3333.0</tendon_stiffness>
      <max_tension>120.0</max_tension>
      <tendon_baseline>0.0</tendon_baseline>
      <use_guided_tendons>true</use_guided_tendons>
      <position_p_gain>8.0</position_p_gain>
      <position_d_gain>0.10</position_d_gain>
      <effort_limit>1.8</effort_limit>
      <tendon_topic>/continuum_arm_cosserat/tendon_cmd</tendon_topic>
      <tip_pose_topic>/continuum_arm_cosserat/tip_pose</tip_pose_topic>
      <tip_link>continuum_arm_cosserat/segment_15</tip_link>

      <!-- command_filter_omega / max_tendon_rate_m_s / solve_rate_hz
           (2026-08 open-loop retune): raw tendon commands only set a
           CommandShaper target now (see plugins/common/CommandShaper.hpp) -
           the BVP is solved against the FILTERED, slowly-ramping length at
           a throttled solve_rate_hz, not re-solved to a hard instantaneous
           target on every command. omega/max_tendon_rate_m_s are the exact
           same values continuum_arm_pcc uses, so both models share one
           open-loop baseline (~2s settle, zero overshoot) and are directly
           comparable. solve_rate_hz=50 matches this repo's usual control-
           loop-rate convention; warm-starting (n0_/m0_,
           guidedNotchesWarmStart_) keeps each throttled solve cheap since
           successive filtered lengths are close together. -->
      <command_filter_omega>2.92</command_filter_omega>
      <max_tendon_rate_m_s>0.06</max_tendon_rate_m_s>
      <solve_rate_hz>50.0</solve_rate_hz>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Joint state feedback (actual, physics-integrated angles), same
         pattern continuum_arm_pcc uses - lets the test script verify the
         arm actually bent the way the tendon command intended. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-state-publisher-system" name="gz::sim::systems::JointStatePublisher">
      <topic>/continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_states</topic>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_1</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_2</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_3</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_4</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_5</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_6</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_7</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_8</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_9</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_10</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_11</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_12</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_13</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_14</joint_name>
      <joint_name>continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_15</joint_name>
    </plugin>

  </model>
</sdf>
